# Conditions

A condition is one thing a rule checks. This page lists every condition in the
rule editor, in the order the add menu shows them, with what it watches, its
settings, and when you would reach for it.

Two things apply to all of them.

**No data means "not true".** If a condition needs something Nocturne has
never received, such as insulin on board from a site with no pump connected, it
is simply never true. It does not raise an error and it does not alert. A rule
built on data you do not have stays silent, so check the data is there before
relying on the rule. The few exceptions are called out below.

**Values are compared exactly as written.** "Below 70" means 69 counts and 70
does not. "At least 2" means 2 counts.

<Callout type="info" title="Not medical advice">

  The example values on this page show how each setting behaves. They are not
  recommendations. Work out your own with your diabetes care team.

</Callout>

## Glucose

### Glucose

The latest CGM reading compared with a value: below it (`<`) or above it (`>`).

- **Settings:** below or above, and a glucose value in your units.
- **Use it for:** the core low and high alerts.
- **Good to know:** "below" and "above" are strict. A rule for "below 70" does
  not fire on a reading of exactly 70. Between readings, the last reading is
  what the rule sees.

### Glucose bucket

The latest reading falls into one of the ranges you tick: very low, low, tight
range, in range, high, very high.

- **Settings:** one or more ranges.
- **Use it for:** rules that should follow your target range, so changing your
  targets changes the alert too.
- **Good to know:** the ranges come from your target range settings. With the
  standard targets they are: very low under 54 mg/dL (3.0 mmol/L), low from 54
  up to 70 (3.0 to 3.9), tight range 70 to 140 (3.9 to 7.8), in range above 140
  up to 180 (7.8 to 10.0), high above 180 up to 250 (10.0 to 13.9), and very
  high above 250 (13.9).

<Callout type="warning" title="In range does not include tight range">

  Each reading falls in exactly one range, so "in range" only covers the part of
  your target range above the tight range: 141 to 180 mg/dL with the standard
  targets. To mean "anywhere in my target range", tick both **tight range** and
  **in range**.

</Callout>

### Predicted glucose

Your loop's forecast of where glucose is heading. The condition is true if
**any** forecast point within the next so many minutes matches.

- **Settings:** a comparison (at least, more than, at most or less than), a glucose value, and how many minutes
  ahead to look.
- **Use it for:** "warn me if my loop thinks I will be under 70 within the next
  30 minutes", before the low arrives.
- **Good to know:** forecasts come from loop apps that upload them, such as
  AAPS and Trio (the oref algorithm). With no forecast the condition is never
  true. A forecast is a guess, and it changes with every loop cycle.

### Rate of change

How fast glucose is moving, in mg/dL (or mmol/L) per minute, as reported by
your CGM with each reading.

- **Settings:** rising or falling, and a rate. The condition is true when
  glucose is moving at least that fast in that direction.
- **Use it for:** catching a fast fall early, usually combined with a glucose
  value (see [Combining conditions](/docs/alerts/combining)).
- **Good to know:** a fall of 2 mg/dL a minute is 0.11 mmol/L a minute, and
  over 15 minutes adds up to 30 mg/dL (1.7 mmol/L). If your CGM or uploader
  does not send a rate with its readings, this condition is never true.

### Trend

The trend arrow, as a rough direction: rising fast, rising, flat, falling,
falling fast.

- **Settings:** one direction.
- **Use it for:** rules that read like the arrow on your CGM app.
- **Good to know:** the direction is worked out from the rate of change. Rising
  fast is 3 mg/dL a minute or more; rising is 1 up to 3; flat is from falling 1
  to rising 1 (a fall of exactly 1 counts as flat); falling is more than 1 up to
  3 (a fall of exactly 3 counts as falling); falling fast is more than 3. Use
  **Rate of change** when you want a precise number.

### Sensor stale

How long since the last CGM reading arrived.

- **Settings:** at least (≥) or more than (>), and a number of minutes.
- **Use it for:** the same job as **Signal loss** below; use whichever reads
  more naturally to you.
- **Good to know:** this is checked every 30 seconds, not only when a reading
  arrives, because the whole point is that none is arriving. On a site that has
  never had a reading, it is never true.

## Insulin

### IOB

Insulin on board: how much insulin your pump or loop reports is still working,
in units.

- **Settings:** a comparison and a number of units.
- **Use it for:** "a low is coming and there is still a lot of insulin working",
  combined with a glucose or forecast condition.

### Temp basal

A temporary basal rate that is running now.

- **Settings:** whether to check the rate itself (units per hour) or the rate as
  a percentage of your scheduled basal, a comparison and a value.
- **Use it for:** noticing a loop that has been running a very high temp basal.
- **Good to know:** when no temp basal is running, the condition is not true.

### Sensitivity

The autosens ratio from AAPS or Trio: how much more or less sensitive to
insulin the loop thinks you are right now. 1 is normal; under 1 means more
sensitive (the loop is giving less insulin), over 1 means less sensitive.

- **Settings:** a comparison and a ratio.
- **Good to know:** only AAPS and Trio report it.

### Time since last bolus

Minutes since the last bolus Nocturne has on record.

- **Settings:** a comparison and a number of minutes.
- **Use it for:** a missed-bolus reminder, together with **Time of day**.
- **Good to know:** unlike most conditions, "no bolus ever recorded" counts as
  a very long time, so "more than 300 minutes" is true on a site with no
  boluses at all.

## Carbs

### COB

Carbs on board: how many grams your loop reports are still being absorbed.

- **Settings:** a comparison and grams.

### Time since last carb

Minutes since the last carb entry. It works like **Time since last bolus**,
including counting "no carbs ever recorded" as a very long time.

## Device

### Reservoir

The insulin left in the pump, in units.

- **Settings:** a comparison and units.
- **Good to know:** some pumps, such as Omnipod, only report "50+" until the
  reservoir drops under 50 units. Nocturne treats "50+" as "at least 50", so it
  can satisfy "more than 20" but never "at most 10".

### Site age

Hours since the last infusion site (cannula) change.

- **Settings:** a comparison and hours. 72 hours is three days.
- **Use it for:** a site change reminder, usually as an Info rule.

### Sensor age

Days since the current CGM sensor was started.

- **Settings:** a comparison and days.

### Tracker age

Time since a tracker you have set up started, or until one is due.

- **Settings:** which tracker, a comparison and minutes.
- **Good to know:** for a tracker that counts down to a scheduled event, the
  time is negative before the event, so "at least minus 30 minutes" is true from
  30 minutes before it is due onwards. A tracker that is not running has no
  age, and the condition is not true.

### Pump battery and Phone battery

The battery level of the pump, or of the phone that uploads your data, in
percent.

- **Settings:** a comparison and a percentage.
- **Good to know:** not true until the pump or phone has reported a battery
  level at least once.

### Loop has stopped

Minutes since your loop last ran a cycle.

- **Settings:** a comparison and minutes.
- **Use it for:** knowing your loop has gone quiet, which often means it has
  lost the pump or the CGM.
- **Good to know:** on a site where a loop has never reported, it is never true.

### Loop not enacting

Minutes since your loop last actually changed what the pump is doing, rather
than just running. A loop that is running but not enacting has usually lost
contact with the pump.

### Signal loss

No CGM reading for a number of minutes.

- **Settings:** minutes.
- **Use it for:** every setup that relies on alerts. When readings stop, every
  other glucose rule goes quiet with them, and this is the rule that tells you.
- **Good to know:** checked every 30 seconds. It clears when the next reading
  arrives. A site that has never had a reading does not raise it.

<AlertSimulator scenario="sensor-gap" rules={["signal-loss"]} />

## Behaviour

### Pump suspended

Whether insulin delivery is suspended.

- **Settings:** whether it is, or is not, and optionally "for at least" a number
  of minutes.
- **Use it for:** a pump left suspended after a shower or a site change.
- **Good to know:** not true until the pump has reported its state at least
  once.

### Override active

Whether an override (a temporary change of targets or settings in your loop)
is running, and optionally for how long.

### Do Not Disturb

Whether Do Not Disturb is on right now, and optionally for how long. Useful for
a rule that tells a carer when Do Not Disturb has been left on.

### Pump mode

The mode your pump or loop reports: Automatic, Limited, Manual, Boost, Ease off,
Sleep, Exercise, Liberty, Suspended or Off.

- **Settings:** a mode, whether it is or is not the current mode, and optionally
  how long.
- **Good to know:** "is not Automatic" is also true when the pump reports no
  mode at all.

## State spans

### State active

Whether a recorded state is running: pump connectivity, override, profile,
exercise, illness, travel, data exclusion or temporary target, optionally a
particular value, and optionally for how long.

### Sleep session

Whether a recorded sleep session is in progress.

## Time

### Time of day

The current time falls inside a window.

- **Settings:** from and to, in 24-hour time, and the time zone.
- **Use it for:** rules that only apply overnight, or only during school hours.
- **Good to know:** the window includes its start and stops just before its
  end: 22:00 to 06:00 includes 22:00 and 05:59, not 06:00. A window that
  crosses midnight works as you would expect. The editor fills in your
  browser's time zone; you can switch it to the time zone on your patient
  record instead.

### Day of week

Today is one of the days you pick, in your time zone.

## Alerts

### Other rule state

Whether another of your rules is firing, unacknowledged or acknowledged, and
optionally for how long.

- **Use it for:** escalation. For example, a rule "Low has been unacknowledged
  for 15 minutes" with a channel that messages a second person. See
  [Example rules](/docs/alerts/recipes#tell-someone-else-if-nobody-responds).
- **Good to know:** "unacknowledged" means firing and not yet acknowledged.
  Snoozing an alert does not acknowledge it, so a snoozed alert still counts as
  unacknowledged. If the other rule is switched off or deleted, this rule is not
  checked at all until it is back.
